n R-leaning state keep him favored, but late-September polling has narrowed the race to a two-to-four-point Republican edge and two surveys show Hinojosa ahead, so the expected margin sits at the competitive end of Lean Republican rather than Likely.", "watch": "If polls keep moving toward Hinojosa through October, or outside spending closes the money gap, this could shift into genuine tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Greg Abbott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican Governor Greg Abbott is seeking another term in a state whose partisan lean favors the GOP by about six points, and he holds a lopsided financial advantage, with roughly $67 million on hand against about $3 million for Democratic state Representative Gina Hinojosa. Those fundamentals anchor the race on the Republican side.\n\nStill, the contest has tightened. Polls through late September show Abbott leading by roughly two to four points, with a Marist survey and a Siena-affiliated poll each putting Hinojosa narrowly ahead. Most expert raters call the race Lean Republican, though Cook and the Silver Bulletin say Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Hinojosa only about a one-in-four chance. The evidence points to a Republican favorite whose margin is narrower than the state's typical Republican edge.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1819, "margin": -4.27}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2694, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2894, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2702, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2293, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -3.3}]}